WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.710
Since its debut in 1989, The Simpsons has
broadcast over 600 episodes, won more than

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:10.040
two dozen Emmys and a Peabody Award, and sold
millions of dollars’ worth of merchandise.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.480
It’s a pop culture juggernaut which means
that there’s a small fortune of lore and

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.860
behind-the-scenes trivia out there about Springfield’s
first family.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.280
Pretty much the only adult Bart Simpson actually
respects is kids’ TV show host Krusty the

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:23.780
Clown.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
When Matt Groening and his writing staff were
developing the show early on, however, someone

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:32.290
floated the concept that Krusty and Homer
were actually going to be the same person.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.840
As Groening told Entertainment Weekly:

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.400
"The original idea behind Krusty the Clown
was that he was Homer in disguise, but Homer

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.540
still couldn’t get any respect from his
son, who worshipped Krusty."

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.960
Unfortunately, it became too complicated a
plot detail to work out once the show was

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.580
really getting off the ground, so the idea
was abandoned but vestiges of the arc remain.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.140
Krusty and Homer have an almost identical
character design Krusty just has different

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.170
hair and clown paint, and they're both voiced
by the same actor.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.960
Later on, the sixth season episode "Homie
the Clown" references the original idea, and

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.780
features Homer becoming a Krusty the Clown
impersonator.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.420
"Wow, I'm sorry I doubted you before, dad."

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.200
"If there has to be a bastardized version
of Krusty, I'm glad it's you."

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.800
The Simpsons' rivalry with President George
H.W. Bush goes back a lot further than the

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.360
neighborhood dispute which erupts in 1996’s
"Two Bad Neighbors."

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.140
In a 1990 interview with People, then-First
Lady Barbara Bush dismissed the show saying,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.520
"It was the dumbest thing I had ever seen."

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.690
In response, the show's writers, under the
guise of Marge Simpson, sent Mrs. Bush a letter

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:34.870
defending the show.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:35.560
“Marge” wrote:

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.100
"I recently read your criticism of my family.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.120
I was deeply hurt.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.500
Heaven knows we're far from perfect and, if
truth be known, maybe just a wee bit short

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.200
of normal; but as Dr. Seuss says, 'a person
is a person.'"

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.040
Barbara apologized, but things didn’t end
there.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.300
Early in his 1992 re-election campaign, President
Bush told the Convention of the National Religious

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.300
Broadcasters,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:02.890
"We are going to keep on trying to strengthen
the American family, to make American families

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.040
a lot more like the Waltons, and a lot less
like the Simpsons."

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.100
A subsequent episode of The Simpsons featured
the family watching a clip of Bush’s speech.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.400
"Hey, we're just like the Waltons.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.300
We're praying for an end to the depression,
too!"

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:20.380
In the Season Three episode "Stark Raving
Dad," Homer is mistakenly sent to a psychiatric

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.870
institution, where he's befriended by a hulking,
balding patient named Leon who identifies

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.160
himself as Michael Jackson.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.920
He certainly had the voice of Michael Jackson
because the real King of Pop lent his voice

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:31.620
to the character.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:32.760
"Who are you?”

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.450
“I’m Michael Jackson of the Jacksons.”

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.720
“I’m Homer Simpson of the Simpsons."

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.860
In the credits, however, he was billed under
the name "John Jay Smith."

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.160
So how did a relatively new TV series land
the biggest pop star in the world?

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.260
Jackson simply phoned up and asked.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.400
A fan of the show, Jackson at first wanted
to play himself, but eventually went with

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.640
the writers when they conceived the character
of Leon.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.220
The Simpsons is one of the few successful,
long-running TV shows to never have generated

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.840
a spinoff but it's not for a lack of trying.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:08.040
In 1994, Matt Groening wrote a script for
a new series that found Krusty the Clown getting

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.460
his own talk show and moving to Los Angeles.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.500
But it wasn't going to be a cartoon Groening
wanted it to be a live-action series, with

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.280
Krusty's voice actor Dan Castellaneta reprising
his role in the flesh.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.380
Eventually, Groening accepted it had to take
the form an animated series instead, because

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.100
this whole thing was just too expensive to
pull off in real life.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.730
Unfortunately, the animated show never saw
the light of day either, after negotiations

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.320
between Groening and Fox stalled… but creativity
prevailed.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.500
Groening's next project was a little show
about Earth in the year 3000.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.180
In late 1988, when The Simpsons was still
just a feature on The Tracey Ullman Show,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.880
producers made a deal with Butterfinger for
a commercial featuring Bart Simpson.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.360
The script called for Bart to compare lunches
with a friend at school, so Simpsons staff

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.920
filed through a series of drawings that Groening
had done for an ultimately unproduced NBC

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.280
Saturday morning cartoon set in a grade school.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.560
The one they picked to use as Bart's foil
was an especially nerdy-looking kid who remained

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.660
unnamed in the commercial.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.420
He’d need a name, however, if he was to
become part of The Simpsons proper.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.100
"Is this the untimely end of Milhouse?

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.920
But Milhouse is my name!

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.260
But I thought I was the only one!

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.620
A pain that I know.all too well."

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.220
Why is he named Milhouse?

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.380
According to Groening,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.500
"I named him Milhouse because I thought that
was the most unfortunate name I could think

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.300
of for a kid."

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:28.760
"This is great!

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.400
And all I've done is enter my name!

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.280
Thrillhouse!"

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.220
The episode "The Itchy &amp; Scratchy &amp; Poochie
Show" parodied the once-common TV practice

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.260
of shows bringing in a cooler, younger, or
out-of-this-world character to freshen things

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:41.760
up.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.780
On The Simpsons, it's Bart's beloved Itchy
&amp; Scratchy cartoon which adds Poochie a sunglasses-wearing

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.320
"rappin' surfer" voiced by Homer.

00:04:49.569 --> 00:04:54.000
Poochie ends up a total flop, with practically
everyone but Homer seeing right through the

00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:58.360
cheap ratings ploy The premise is paralleled
in the Simpsons household, which suddenly

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.110
includes a never-before-seen, sunglasses-wearing
dude named Roy, who at the end of the episode

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:03.680
moves out.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.650
"Good news, everybody!

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.040
I'm moving into my own place with two sexy
ladies!"

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.740
"Well, I guess this is goodbye, Roy."

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.780
Fox had recently tried to do the same thing
to The Simpsons.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.960
Before the eighth season began, Fox executives
asked then-showrunners Bill Oakley and Josh

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.160
Weinstein to add a new, fresh face to The
Simpsons family.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.660
The writers gave them Roy and Poochie instead.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.660
As for Poochie, well, he had the greatest
send-off ever...

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.460
“I have to go now.  My planet needs me.."

